  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/21E814">Remark <span data-tag="21E814">6.5.2.21</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos. The collection of all $\infty $-connective morphisms in $\calX $ is saturated. It follows from Proposition <a href="/tag/256E42" data-tag="256E42">5.5.5.7</a> that there exists a factorization system $(S_ L, S_ R)$ on $\calX $, where $S_ L$ is the collection of all $\infty $-connective morphisms in $\calX $. We will say that a morphism in $\calX $ is <em>hypercomplete</em> if it belongs to $S_ R$. Unwinding the definitions (and using the fact that a morphism in $\calX _{/Y}$ is $\infty $-connective if and only if its image in $\calX $ is $\infty $-connective), we conclude that a morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ is hypercomplete if and only if it is hypercomplete when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-topos $\calX _{/Y}$ (see §<a href="/tag/B2D503" data-tag="B2D503">6.5.2</a>). </p>
  <p>Using Proposition <a href="/tag/969C8B" data-tag="969C8B">5.2.8.6</a>, we deduce that the collection of hypercomplete morphisms in $\calX $ is stable under limits and the formation of pullback squares. </p>
